From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bpereira <bpereira@cloudflare.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 11:07:30 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Encode RGBA images with wasm SIMD128

On wasm32 with simd128, RGBA input takes a vectorized path:

- color conversion runs four pixels per i32 lane;
- each 8x8 block stays in registers from sampling through the islow
  DCT and quantization, then lands in zigzag order via byte shuffles
  (a new `Operations::encode_block` hook, whose default keeps the
  get_block + fdct + quantize_block sequence);
- rows the encoder only reads luma from (4:2:0 chroma skips every other
  row) skip chroma conversion via a hidden, defaulted
  `ImageBuffer::fill_buffers_first_only`;
- horizontally subsampled chroma reads only the even columns of a row,
  so rows that feed chroma convert Cb/Cr for the even pixels of each
  16-pixel chunk and copy each value into the odd pixel after it,
  through a hidden, defaulted `ImageBuffer::fill_buffers_even_others`.
  The scalar remainder still converts every pixel, so the last column
  keeps its own chroma whenever the width needs padding;
- a block whose 64 samples are all equal has an islow DCT of exactly
  64*v at DC and zero elsewhere, so only its DC is quantized. Page
  frames are mostly such blocks (backgrounds, chroma under gray text).

The Huffman AC writer walks only the nonzero coefficients of a bitmask
(SIMD on wasm, a scalar fold elsewhere); the gap between set bits is
the zero run. Its bit accumulator is a u64 on every target, so 32-bit
targets (wasm32) flush 8 bytes at a time.

The interleaved loop no longer zeroes a coefficient block per block
(encode_block overwrites all of it), and sample_rows no longer goes
through an array::from_fn closure that did not inline.

Every stage uses the scalar integer formulas, so output is
byte-identical to the scalar encoder (checked at q30-q100 on text,
dashboard and synthetic frames). wasm32 simd128 under V8, 1280x720
page frames at q80: the flat-block skip alone took encoding from 2.7
to 2.1 ms (text) and from 2.25 to 1.55 ms (dashboard).
